<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alauddin al-Kahar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1571">1571</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Alauddin al-Qahhar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alauddin al-Kahar was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> from about 1537 to 1571 and made it a regional power. He sought military aid from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which sent gunners and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> in the 1560s, and repeatedly attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-malacca">Portuguese Malacca</a> as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batak">Batak</a> lands. His reign secured Aceh's place in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> trade and its links with the Muslim powers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alauddin-al-kahar.jpg" alt="Alauddin al-Kahar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Si Gam · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jirat_S%C3%B4leutan_Al-Qahhar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alauddin was the second son of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-mughayat-syah">Ali Mughayat Syah</a>, founder of the Aceh Sultanate. After Ali's death in 1530 the eldest son, Salahuddin, took the throne but governed ineptly, while the queen mother held great influence and appointed a regent, Raja Bungsu, who bore a green payung, or parasol, as a sign of authority. Alauddin meanwhile governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudera-pasai-sultanate">Samudra Pasai</a>, conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> in 1524. In about 1537 or 1539 he staged a coup, killed Raja Bungsu and imprisoned Salahuddin and the queen mother, Sitt Hur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His campaigns began in 1539 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batak">Batak people</a> to the south; the Portuguese traveller Fernão Mendes Pinto says the sultan attacked their king after he refused to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, though his account is doubtful. Alauddin then attacked the Aru Kingdom and killed its ruler, but the army of the Johor Sultanate forced him to withdraw in around 1540. Acehnese tradition remembers him as the ruler who divided society into lineage groups, kaum or sukeë, though the attribution is uncertain.</p><p>Portuguese Melaka, held since 1511, was his principal enemy, since control of the trade through the Melaka Straits was at stake. A night attack in 1547 failed when his fleet was defeated at the Perlis River. In 1564 he sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> and brought its sultan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-riayat-shah-ii">Alauddin Riayat Shah II</a> of Johor, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>, where he died. In January 1568 he led 15,000 troops and 400 Ottoman soldiers with 200 bronze <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> against Melaka, but the Portuguese beat them back, and his eldest son Abdullah of Aru was killed.</p><p>Around 1561–1562 he sent envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a>. A route for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> trade from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> had developed from about 1530, avoiding the Portuguese strongholds, which gave both powers a common interest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a> equipped a fleet of 15 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>, which had to be recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, but two vessels with munitions and military experts reached Aceh. A sea battle near Aceh's main port in 1570 still ended with heavy Acehnese losses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alauddin died in 1571, as the inscription on his grave shows, and was succeeded by his son, Ali Ri'ayat Syah I. The chronicle Bustanus Salatin names his sons, among them Abdul Jalil, whom he had killed for excessive ambition and who was the grandfather of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iskandar-muda">Iskandar Muda</a>. Though he never took Melaka, his reign saw the beginning of the high tide of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>'s power.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alauddin al-Kahar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
